**Default `JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter` behavior:**
- Reads `scope` or `scp` claim
- Creates `SCOPE_read`, `SCOPE_write` authorities

**The silent mismatch:**
- If your auth server uses `roles: ["ADMIN", "USER"]`, Spring doesn't recognize it
- Empty authority set, but authentication succeeds
- All `hasRole()` checks fail silently

**Fix with a `JwtAuthenticationConverter` bean:**

```java
@Bean
public JwtAuthenticationConverter jwtAuthenticationConverter() {
    JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter grantedAuthoritiesConverter =
        new JwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter();
    grantedAuthoritiesConverter.setAuthoritiesClaimName("roles");
    grantedAuthoritiesConverter.setAuthorityPrefix("ROLE_");

    JwtAuthenticationConverter jwtAuthConverter = new JwtAuthenticationConverter();
    jwtAuthConverter.setJwtGrantedAuthoritiesConverter(grantedAuthoritiesConverter);
    return jwtAuthConverter;
}
```

**Spring auto-detects this bean:**
- No additional wiring needed
- `hasRole("ADMIN")` works because converter creates `ROLE_ADMIN` from the claim value `ADMIN`

**For complex claim structures — implement the converter directly:**

```java
Converter<Jwt, Collection<GrantedAuthority>> custom = jwt -> {
    List<String> roles = jwt.getClaimAsStringList("roles");
    List<String> perms = jwt.getClaimAsStringList("permissions");
    return Stream.concat(
        roles.stream().map(r -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority("ROLE_" + r)),
        perms.stream().map(p -> new SimpleGrantedAuthority("PERM_" + p))
    ).collect(Collectors.toList());
};
```

**Trust model:**
- Converter runs after signature validation — JWT is already trusted
- Resource server trusts whatever roles auth server puts in the JWT
- Auth server's security is the root of trust
